#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039b1404-c3c5-5f0d-9a9a-7558551fd64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280986} {:answer/id #uuid "c34dde87-5094-5eee-91dc-6aa1b724f521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280987} {:answer/id #uuid "f6231a12-ab5e-5bbd-b99c-a535dd85e364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280988} {:answer/id #uuid "2adaaddc-1b23-5ecc-bb68-50ad35a9cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5b2501-b5d5-505e-b2bc-8081f1f55e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adaaddc-1b23-5ecc-bb68-50ad35a9cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac17ce1a-7905-54b1-913b-6084706ce9ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a933fd6d-6a35-5eb0-9d87-5a41bdcc4a21" :question/math-academy-id "q-118779" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28649c78-5975-5e5b-9f64-24e6e19800cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280993} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c06e4d-4cfb-5018-9420-a1c73a3667b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280994} {:answer/id #uuid "9f211831-069a-570b-83ed-b75cee7516da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280995} {:answer/id #uuid "66d25487-68f6-5014-8db4-e0cb08a0de0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280996} {:answer/id #uuid "b29801d2-6483-514e-9099-aa269d9816f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66d25487-68f6-5014-8db4-e0cb08a0de0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280996} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d987eb-903e-5449-b204-80f83227cb1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84db76b0-471c-51dc-bdd6-2d39dffa4f28" :question/math-academy-id "q-118781" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84525e64-3770-5710-800d-17ca14cb1df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281000} {:answer/id #uuid "e51c41a2-5a38-50c1-b64e-c02234a5c23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281001} {:answer/id #uuid "511a2a26-f405-5752-9647-cc01b576f3e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281002} {:answer/id #uuid "08307e15-cafd-5fa9-b4ce-b10cd892d7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281003} {:answer/id #uuid "24974ecf-0ec7-5319-af8e-c5ecd324b478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e51c41a2-5a38-50c1-b64e-c02234a5c23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f458c836-e756-5985-b647-d7229c4f9fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9102c868-96ec-5673-8dcc-be6cc7ee008d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118787" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "905c956b-71ca-53d4-96fe-817ce32a0fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281007} {:answer/id #uuid "3add1308-38cf-5b33-b911-8b52a18a6451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281008} {:answer/id #uuid "5d533b47-95eb-5b1a-92f1-d16bec403fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281009} {:answer/id #uuid "66878d39-e481-5c06-bc31-bdbfe95200e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281010} {:answer/id #uuid "7f87b4c2-55f6-5a21-b85e-5eacbf4399f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66878d39-e481-5c06-bc31-bdbfe95200e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281010} :answer-field/id #uuid "866fad75-1b84-5f9d-b6e3-33fb6596644c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4db518e4-03ec-5f0b-bee4-e745f4578bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-118790" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70d61d2a-27c7-5a37-af06-55a203b16f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281014} {:answer/id #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015} {:answer/id #uuid "113ab132-b40f-5ab4-be6a-6c9ba0884b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281016} {:answer/id #uuid "e94997a8-0a2e-5b11-84a6-6d30752c74f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281017} {:answer/id #uuid "777be402-ac0f-53da-98ec-0b10ae621300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015} :answer-field/id #uuid "40012c1d-87cf-5109-9a23-6aca313e1115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90a18919-f815-5b77-9ac2-909415c66f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3401dc1c-be3b-5450-86fa-b713939750f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281021} {:answer/id #uuid "d5066b58-78d8-5140-a9a4-8ca339b47771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281022} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6e971a-90bd-5d56-bdbc-dbb5935fdfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281023} {:answer/id #uuid "4b58bfba-b2d3-55d1-9844-ac232b42d948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281024} {:answer/id #uuid "a77cbbdd-237a-5e08-8415-610597bce1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3401dc1c-be3b-5450-86fa-b713939750f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281021} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e20f1e-782b-5788-bac7-b40b22e346c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c091681-9ae6-5d3e-9149-c42e1bb6941d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4a44460-7381-5aef-af91-96d09f592d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281028} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd9cbc5-5cc6-555c-8b8d-e057aa50623d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281029} {:answer/id #uuid "1db39c90-0021-5858-9a36-892f0cfc695f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281030} {:answer/id #uuid "3df41c3d-5ac4-52f6-a7bf-f5ab1a58da23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281031} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8d5d2a-7cdc-5f1c-aea0-e15081dbc3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db39c90-0021-5858-9a36-892f0cfc695f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281030} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ee4e6b-9bed-5358-88b0-838325134f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6041e3-4776-511a-b5c9-9dd819e86bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a362f3bc-f3de-5a24-8bdc-2e5de1afd3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165811} {:answer/id #uuid "b82ea3d8-9cf2-5b0c-9f5c-edae9c45197c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165812} {:answer/id #uuid "bed30f47-0163-5d27-b917-d60e8135d88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165813} {:answer/id #uuid "ffdde2f1-d7a5-5e8e-b1fe-2c3df6a68b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165814} {:answer/id #uuid "3932c714-868e-5af6-8f57-faa5aef0076b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed30f47-0163-5d27-b917-d60e8135d88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165813} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7830fd-e7fa-50b5-a61e-222206b0390d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6d57756-51a0-53da-bab4-6debeaf1e4ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-95982" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{3}y=5{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ which has order $2.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce4acdd-0589-54c7-822a-272cb00980e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165829} {:answer/id #uuid "56387554-f843-5d1d-b7bb-91da91584863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165830} {:answer/id #uuid "6740aa00-f0bc-57d2-a30a-68e33996886b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165831} {:answer/id #uuid "6e83c890-0b59-5c1a-8da0-0162d2823067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165832} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fc708-37b8-5fbb-9d3a-5052130cb2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ce4acdd-0589-54c7-822a-272cb00980e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165829} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4ceaf6-15ec-5b6b-8a59-7a6136b14b74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c01fd7ca-ed83-5e17-8036-cd03a49c52f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95985" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f8ea16-f9cd-595d-839d-df65010a5149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281035} {:answer/id #uuid "8708086d-d7e2-5311-97cc-d59dd5ce5ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281036} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9d0f75-0bd5-51f7-970d-2cdf9b4bd698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281037} {:answer/id #uuid "81c7b091-9e59-5e81-aa07-6c9a58b0133b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281038} {:answer/id #uuid "259e1a9a-b4c6-5859-b8e9-e7ff7a430ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81c7b091-9e59-5e81-aa07-6c9a58b0133b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281038} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcc026b-82d2-5846-98c8-b2a64ac1522f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0670947c-d413-52e3-9abb-4b0fd46d0cd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96663" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98b22c21-b0a7-5862-8cfd-1e76c5e5452f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281042} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc88690-1ced-5d6a-bf7e-b0b15cf9fed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281043} {:answer/id #uuid "96e075c5-6062-5628-87a5-6d937f4cf315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281044} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed9d894-4445-5f33-91f7-c658ad8bc60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281045} {:answer/id #uuid "4a64863a-0b41-571e-b97d-3f02151d34cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ed9d894-4445-5f33-91f7-c658ad8bc60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281045} :answer-field/id #uuid "81fce6b9-aaa9-52f2-8292-368cf3a81fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2516b57-9368-5a93-af6f-1abc7e22553a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96672" :question/problem "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}{x}^{3}-2y{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2y=2{y}^{′}$\n- $4{y}^{‴}x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9efb605-ea2e-5276-951e-45dfc94d677e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281049} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b8be8e-1100-512a-8661-62dfd15d9af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281050} {:answer/id #uuid "b9037e85-690a-57c7-810c-89240a8faf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281051} {:answer/id #uuid "ced67b4c-1e73-5217-a34d-7acc4b3044ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddfbb2f-742c-578c-9479-00d783882ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced67b4c-1e73-5217-a34d-7acc4b3044ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052} :answer-field/id #uuid "8661cba3-7048-5b7f-9533-d880c35c415e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b527fd3f-b1f2-54f1-b985-21a0b1fa81ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8ac46e-b347-5f0e-909a-dcff80c5eab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281056} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fec451-4fb0-5dbe-ab3c-b5a486cef109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281057} {:answer/id #uuid "6eeba983-720f-56dc-8b71-b7e0f41f1ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281058} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ec787f-53e6-5770-9220-2bf0503243ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281059} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa2c58b-83b1-5a1f-82ce-1b76a28a1da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aa2c58b-83b1-5a1f-82ce-1b76a28a1da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281060} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c44b9f-1895-57d1-b493-bf687d31b16e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21877f7e-3309-5578-9c06-511e21f96e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-96713" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c98a91c-3913-54f1-b523-a0110cd22753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281063} {:answer/id #uuid "54334491-13f1-5986-89d2-27af10a558a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281064} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7628d8-e294-5ccb-b089-d4ffe9f9b156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281065} {:answer/id #uuid "fbadf60a-49c0-515c-be47-e5818be7829b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281066} {:answer/id #uuid "684496cd-8bc5-55f5-ab37-59d661096878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54334491-13f1-5986-89d2-27af10a558a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281064} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9570b8-6ef7-57bd-9b36-2588c38d2c8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3ca412b-50e9-5db7-a51c-5819f837524c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8fcc14-3b71-5da6-b5dd-7babd909bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166171} {:answer/id #uuid "cc48ca6b-4aa3-5db8-bea8-d0c431202662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} {:answer/id #uuid "38f746bf-71f6-5862-b734-46ca998885c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166173} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5b041-244e-5910-a46e-1d2d8b63ba6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166174} {:answer/id #uuid "61ba28ba-32e1-59ce-87b7-bf847008593c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc48ca6b-4aa3-5db8-bea8-d0c431202662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a53d5f-04a9-5740-b2e4-3c2ac8d9a527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
